# Documentation edits

Applies to README, `docs/`, CHANGELOG, ADRs, doc comments, and any `.md`/`.mdx` file.

- Change only what the task needs. Do not rewrite, reformat, or "polish" unrelated text.
- Prefer deleting a sentence to adding one. Prefer a bullet to a paragraph.
- Every sentence must add a fact, a rule, or a step. Remove filler, repetition, and marketing.
- Write for a human new to the project: short sentences, concrete nouns, one idea per paragraph.
- Keep the file's language, tone, and structure. Match the surrounding docs.
- Do not rename headings or anchors unless the task requires it: links may break.
- Use an example only when it removes ambiguity. Keep examples runnable and minimal.
- Do not document obvious code or repeat what the code already says.
- When behavior changes, update the docs that mention it; do not add a new document.
- Never invent commands, paths, or features. Check the code or ask.

Avoid AI-speak: "seamlessly", "leverage", "robust", "comprehensive", "it's important to note", "in today's world".

## Examples

Bad:
> Scrib is a powerful, modern, local-first solution that seamlessly leverages state-of-the-art AI to empower your workflow.

Good:
> Scrib records a meeting and transcribes it locally. Audio and text stay on your Mac.

Bad:
> ## Installation
> First, make sure you have installed all the required prerequisites. Then...

Good:
> ## Install
> ```sh
> cargo install --path .
> ```
